      "id": "ex-em-dielectric-sphere-example",
      "section": "Example",
      "text": "Let the laboratory frame and units be those of [[def-em-lab-geometry]] and [[def-em-si-conventions]], with spatial coordinates $x=(x_1,x_2,x_3)$, $z=x_3$, $r=|x|$ and the applied field along $e_3$. Fix $a>0$ with $\\overline B_a\\subseteq\\Omega$, so that the interface $S=\\{r=a\\}$ below lies in the laboratory region, a field magnitude $E_0>0$ and constants $\\varepsilon_{\\mathrm{in}},\\varepsilon_{\\mathrm{out}}\\in(0,\\infty)$ (the interior and exterior permittivities of the elementary branch of [[post-em-material-closure]], the material being at rest in the laboratory frame), and put\n\n$$C:=\\frac{3\\varepsilon_{\\mathrm{out}}}{\\varepsilon_{\\mathrm{in}}+2\\varepsilon_{\\mathrm{out}}},\\qquad d:=\\frac{E_0a^3(\\varepsilon_{\\mathrm{in}}-\\varepsilon_{\\mathrm{out}})}{\\varepsilon_{\\mathrm{in}}+2\\varepsilon_{\\mathrm{out}}}.$$\n\nConsider the time-independent potentials\n\n$$\\varphi_{\\mathrm{in}}(x):=-C\\,E_0\\,z\\quad(r<a),\\qquad \\varphi_{\\mathrm{out}}(x):=-E_0\\,z+\\frac{d\\,z}{r^3}\\quad(r>a),$$\n\nwith fields $E:=-\\nabla\\varphi$ and $D:=\\varepsilon_{\\mathrm{in}}E$ inside, $D:=\\varepsilon_{\\mathrm{out}}E$ outside ($E$ being of the class of [[def-em-fields]] restricted to this time-independent configuration). Write $S=\\{r=a\\}$ and $n=e_r$ for the outward normal. Then:\n\n**(a) [Harmonicity]** $\\Delta\\varphi_{\\mathrm{in}}=0$ on $r<a$ and $\\Delta\\varphi_{\\mathrm{out}}=0$ on $r>a$ in the classical sense of [[def-laplacian-of-a-c2-function]], and the potentials and their first derivatives extend continuously to the closed sides of $S$.\n\n**(b) [Interface conditions]** With one-sided traces at $r=a$, $\\varphi_{\\mathrm{in}}=\\varphi_{\\mathrm{out}}$ and $\\varepsilon_{\\mathrm{in}}\\,\\partial_r\\varphi_{\\mathrm{in}}=\\varepsilon_{\\mathrm{out}}\\,\\partial_r\\varphi_{\\mathrm{out}}$ on $S$; equivalently the tangential electric field and the normal component of $D$ are continuous. These electric matching identities hold independently of any magnetic completion. In addition, declare the static completion $B=H=0$, $\\rho_f=J_f=0$, with any constant symmetric positive-definite permeability and the zero-conductivity branch $\\sigma=0$, $J_{\\mathrm{ext}}=0$ of [[post-em-material-closure]]. The four fields then satisfy all four thin-interface macroscopic equations distributionally with $\\sigma_f=K_f=0$, and the joint one-sided regularity at the compact sphere verifies the hypotheses of [[pthm-em-material-stationary-jumps]]. Assume its inherited $\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$ convention ([[def-countable-choice]]) for this distribution/surface reading.\n\n**(c) [Asymptotics]** $\\varphi_{\\mathrm{out}}(x)+E_0z=dz/r^3$ and $\\nabla(\\varphi_{\\mathrm{out}}+E_0z)=O(r^{-3})$ as $r\\to\\infty$: the difference from the applied uniform potential decays like $r^{-2}$ and its gradient like $r^{-3}$.\n\n**(d) [Uniqueness in the declared class]** Assume Countable Choice ([[def-countable-choice]]), as required by the Green and harmonic derivative-estimate interfaces used in this branch. Let $(\\tilde\\varphi_{\\mathrm{in}},\\tilde\\varphi_{\\mathrm{out}})$ be $C^2$ on the closed ball $\\overline{B_a}$ respectively on every closed exterior region $\\{a\\le r\\le R\\}$, harmonic on $r<a$ and $r>a$, satisfying the same interface conditions (b), and satisfying $|\\tilde\\varphi_{\\mathrm{out}}+E_0z|\\le M/r$ for some $M$ and all sufficiently large $r$. Then $\\tilde\\varphi_{\\mathrm{in}}=\\varphi_{\\mathrm{in}}$ and $\\tilde\\varphi_{\\mathrm{out}}=\\varphi_{\\mathrm{out}}$. Hence the displayed pair is the unique solution of the dielectric-sphere transmission problem in that class, existence being exhibited explicitly; the uniqueness proof uses only the Green identity, the matching conditions and the decay, with no maximum principle.\n\n**(e) [Fields, polarisation and induced dipole]** On $r<a$ the field is uniform, $E=C E_0e_3$ with $D=\\varepsilon_{\\mathrm{in}}CE_0e_3$; outside, the field is that of the applied uniform field plus the field of a point dipole at the origin with moment $p=4\\pi\\varepsilon_{\\mathrm{out}}\\,d\\,e_3$; the interior polarisation is $P_{\\mathrm{in}}=(\\varepsilon_{\\mathrm{in}}-\\varepsilon_0)CE_0e_3$ and the bound surface charge density on $S$ is $\\sigma_b=n\\cdot(P_{\\mathrm{in}}-P_{\\mathrm{out}})$, the singular coefficient of $\\rho_b=-\\operatorname{div}P$ at $S$ (with the exterior polarisation $P_{\\mathrm{out}}=(\\varepsilon_{\\mathrm{out}}-\\varepsilon_0)E_{\\mathrm{out}}$).\n\n**(f) [Regime and scope]** The computation is a quasistatic result of the declared linear branch at $\\sigma_f=0$; using $p$ as a dynamic wave polarisability of the sphere requires a separate quasistatic/dipole approximation and is not asserted here. The example asserts no material data and no empirical claim."
